{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794460316,"nonce":"/r404uxnw6sJ5FQJX3fw7eJr1IQ=","title":"x","message":"Today the build library runs rootless BuildKit with `--oci-worker-no-process-sandbox`. Each Dockerfile `RUN` shares the build container's PID namespace and `/proc`, so it can see buildctl, rootlesskit and buildkitd. Only the kernel's capability rule keeps it from reading them (see 6f54105).\n\nWith the process sandbox, each `RUN` gets its own PID namespace and a fresh `/proc`. It can't see those processes at all, which is a second barrier independent of the capability rule. After the build/push split, the build step holds no push credential. The sandbox then protects the cache-write identity and stops a `RUN` from signalling or interfering with buildkitd.\n\nWhat it needs on the build Pod:\n- `hostUsers: false` (user namespaces for Pods; UserNamespacesSupport, containerd 2.x, idmap mounts, kernel \u003e= 6.3).\n- `procMount: Unmasked` on the build container, so BuildKit can mount a fresh /proc for each RUN.\n- Pod Security: check which level admits `procMount: Unmasked` with `hostUsers: false` on the cluster's Kubernetes version.\n\nTo do:\n1. Live test on the pilot's EKS 1.35 / AL2023 (kernel 6.12.113) nodes: whether a Pod with `hostUsers: false` and `procMount: Unmasked` is admitted, and whether rootless buildkitd without `--oci-worker-no-process-sandbox` builds in it. Record containerd version and the Pod Security level.\n2. If it works: a core security primitive for this (likely an option on `security.sandbox`), with the Pod fields in the executor. Make the process sandbox the library default and keep `--oci-worker-no-process-sandbox` only as an operator-chosen fallback.\n3. A kind or e2e case: a Dockerfile RUN lists /proc and must not see buildkitd.\n\nNot done until the live test on EKS has run.","files":null}]}